course: arts
lesson: 38
title: Praise and criticism: giving and taking feedback
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Feedback is what people tell you about your work so you can make it better, and the useful kind names one thing that works and one clear thing to improve.
explain: "It's rubbish" only hurts, and "Amazing!" about everything soon means nothing; "the faces are funny, but I can't read the words" actually helps, like a map that shows you where to go next.
explain: Taking feedback is a skill too: listen to the end, say thanks, and decide later what to use, instead of answering every point with "yeah but".
explain: Not every comment is feedback: a stranger online writing "cringe" is not trying to help, so you can block, report and tell an adult, and listen to people who want you to improve.
explain: People say it their own way: "ken" in Scotland means you know, "tamping" in Wales means furious, "arvo" in Australia means afternoon, "boss" in Liverpool means great, and "ta" in Britain means thanks.
fact: Useful feedback is kind, specific and helpful: it names something that works and one clear thing to improve.
fact: In the "Austin's Butterfly" story told by the teacher Ron Berger, a young boy drew a butterfly six times, and kind, specific feedback from classmates made each draft better.
fact: A draft is a version of your work that you plan to improve.
fact: Praise that is the same for everything stops feeling real; praise that names something, like "the way you did the shadows", tells you what works.
fact: Parents often praise everything because they love you, even when the praise is not specific.
fact: "Two stars and a wish", used in many UK schools, means two good things and one thing to improve; it is not a score out of five.
fact: Many marks on your work usually mean the teacher took it seriously; asking which mark matters most turns a scary page into a plan.
fact: Mean comments online from strangers are not feedback; you can block, report and ignore them, and tell an adult if it gets bad.
fact: You can be kind and honest at once: "I love the colours, the horse's legs could be longer."
fact: Singing "flat" means slightly lower than the right note, and singers often cannot hear it in their own voice, so a teacher's ear or a recording helps.
fact: Critics write reviews with reasons; you can read their reasons and still disagree, as taste is personal.
fact: Taking feedback well means listening first, not arguing back straight away, and deciding later what to use.
exercise: Ewan's mate calls his comic "pure rubbish, ken" ("ken" means you know). Why does it not help? | It does not say what to fix | Because comics cannot be improved by anyone | Because only teachers are allowed to judge drawings | Because Ewan's mate is from a different town
exercise: What makes feedback useful? | One thing that works and one clear thing to improve | Telling the person everything that is wrong at once | Only saying "amazing" so nobody is upset | Being as harsh as possible so it sinks in
exercise: Mason says six drafts of one butterfly is "so extra". What did the Austin's Butterfly story show? | Kind, specific feedback made each draft better | The first drawing was the best one all along | Drawing the same thing six times is a waste | Only grown-ups can really improve their drawings with feedback from others
exercise: What is a draft? | A version of work you plan to improve | The final copy that goes on the wall | A cold wind coming through a window | A mark the teacher gives out of ten
exercise: Valentyna's mum says "Amazing!" about every drawing. What would help Valentyna more? | Asking "which part do you like most?" | Telling her mum to stop praising her | Throwing away the drawings her mum likes | Showing her drawings only to strangers online
exercise: Why does Valentyna's mum praise everything? | She loves her | Mums are not able to see mistakes in drawings | She has been told to by the school | She is secretly hiding that she dislikes them
exercise: Owen gets "two stars and a wish" and feels "tamping" (furious). What does it mean? | Two good points and one thing to work on | His poem scored two out of five | His poem was the worst in the class | He has to make a wish and hope his next poem is luckier
exercise: Zhang Wei's music is covered in red pencil. What is the wisest move? | Ask which correction matters most and start there | Decide the teacher hates him and quit piano | Rub out all the marks before his parents see | Play the piece much faster so the teacher cannot spot the mistakes
exercise: A stranger comments "cringe, stop dancing" on Isla's video. What should she do? | Block or report it and talk to an adult | Stop dancing for ever because of one comment | Reply with something even meaner | Post the comment everywhere so people see
exercise: Ibrahim tells his sister her horse is great when he thinks it looks like a dog. What could he say instead? | "I love the colours; the legs could be longer" | "It looks like a dog, start again" | "It is perfect, do not change a thing" | Nothing, and walk away without looking
exercise: What does "flat" mean in singing? | Slightly lower than the right note | Singing much too loudly in the chorus | Singing while lying down on the floor | Slightly faster than the rest of the band
exercise: Why is a coach's ear useful for a singer? | Singers often cannot hear small pitch mistakes in their own voice | Coaches have better ears than everybody else in the world | Singers are not allowed to listen to themselves sing | A coach can sing every song better than the singer can
exercise: Emma says critics who disliked her film are "haters". What is fairer? | Read their reasons, then decide for herself | Critics are always right, so the film is bad | Never read any reviews of anything ever | Critics only review films they have not seen
exercise: Arjun answers every point with "Yeah but...". What should he do instead? | Listen fully, say thanks, decide later | Argue harder so his friend gives up | Stop asking anyone for feedback ever | Only ask people who will say it is perfect
exercise: Priya tells her teammate "dead good, but you came in late after the bridge". Why is it good feedback? | It is kind and names one clear problem | It lists every single mistake in the song | It only praises, so nobody gets upset | It tells the teammate to leave the band
exercise: Which comment is the most helpful? | "The faces are funny, but I can't read the words" | "It's rubbish" | "It's fine, I suppose" | "Amazing, amazing, amazing"
test: Your mate goes, "Harsh is honest, so I'll just say it's trash." What is the best answer? | Harsh and vague gives no useful information | He is right, the harsher the better | Honest feedback must always be rude | Saying "trash" is the most specific feedback
test: What would happen if Austin had stopped after his first butterfly? | He would never have seen how much better it could get | His first drawing would have improved by itself | His classmates would have drawn it for him | The butterfly would have turned out perfect anyway, as first tries always do
test: A kid from Phoenix says six drafts is "so extra" ("extra" means over the top). Which is true? | Drafts with feedback can make work much better | Your first try always shows your best | Only lazy people need more than one draft | Drafts are only for maths homework
test: In a cartoon, a teacher's red marks turn into monsters that chase a boy. What are marks really for? | Helping you find and fix mistakes | Showing that the teacher dislikes you | Scaring pupils into practising more | Making the page look more colourful
test: Owen thinks two stars means "only two out of five". What is the correction? | It means two good points and one wish to work on | It is a score out of five, so he did badly | Two stars means he must repeat the year | It means two people in the class liked it and everybody else did not
test: Isla cries "all arvo" ("arvo" means afternoon) over a mean comment. Whose feedback is worth listening to? | People who know her and want her to improve | Every stranger who comments on any video | Only the meanest comments, as they are honest | Nobody at all, ever
test: Jamie says, "Flat? I'm boss at singing" ("boss" means great). What would help him check? | Recording himself and listening back | Singing louder so it cannot be flat | Asking a friend who cannot sing | Ignoring the coach completely
test: Ibrahim's mum asks "why lie?". Which is true? | You can be kind and honest at once | Kind people always tell small lies | Honest people are never kind | Lies are fine if your sister smiles
test: Emma writes "critics are haters, period" ("period" means end of discussion). Which is fairer? | Critics give reasons, and she can disagree with reasons | Critics are paid to hate every film | She is wrong to like the film at all | Taste in films is exactly the same for everyone, so critics must be wrong
test: Arjun's friend says, "Then why are you asking, re?" ("re" is a friendly call). What has Arjun been doing? | Arguing back instead of listening | Listening carefully to every point | Saying thank you too many times | Writing down every comment in a notebook
test: Your teammate says "ta, I'll count it in next time" ("ta" means thanks). What made the feedback work? | It was kind, clear and about one moment | It listed every mistake in the whole song | It was shouted in front of the audience | It was sent to her by a stranger online
exam: Identify the principal characteristic of feedback that assists improvement. | It is specific, naming one strength and one point to improve | It is severe and general so that it is remembered | It consists solely of unqualified praise | It enumerates every defect simultaneously so that none may be overlooked
exam: Which of the following most accurately characterises the method "two stars and a wish"? | Two commendations and one suggestion for improvement | A rating of two points out of a maximum of five | A penalty imposed for unsatisfactory work | A wish expressed by the teacher for a better pupil
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning a singer's perception of their own pitch. | Small errors are frequently inaudible to the singer | Every singer perceives every error without assistance | Pitch cannot be assessed by any listener | Recordings invariably distort the pitch of a voice
exam: Ascertain the conduct most appropriate upon receipt of feedback which one has requested. | Attentive listening, thanks, and later consideration | Immediate contradiction of every point raised | Refusal to request feedback on any future occasion | Solicitation of praise from persons who agree
